Q: eCharts Tooltip with Pointer A: <p><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/14553216/mawnir-fas">Mawnir fas</a>, in the echarts v5, this problem can be fixed by code below:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  tooltip: {
    trigger: 'item',
    position: 'right',
    appendTo: 'body',
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>It looks like this:</p>
<p><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/FyPsZUOV.png" alt="enter image description here" />.</p>

Q: Matching an empty input field using CSS A: <p>I realize this is a very old thread, but things have changed a bit since and it did help me find the right combination of things I needed to get my problem fixed. So I thought I'd share what I did.</p>
<p>The problem was I needed to have the same css applied for an optional input if it was filled, as I had for a filled required. The css used the psuedo class :valid which applied the css on the optional input also when not filled.</p>
<p>This is how I fixed it;</p>
<p>HTML</p>
<pre><code>&lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; required=&quot;required&quot;&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; placeholder=&quot;&quot;&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>CSS</p>
<pre><code>input:required:valid {
    ....
}
input:optional::not(:placeholder-shown){
    ....
}
</code></pre>

Q: iOS 26 broken view hierarchy on Menu A: <p>This error seems to happen for menus being shown inside the content of the page. It doesn't happen for menus in a toolbar.</p>
<ul>
<li>A possible workaround is to apply <code>.glassEffect()</code> to the menu.</li>
<li>To improve the styling of the button, you might want to apply a button style too, such as <code>.glass</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>When you try this and the menu is dismissed, the button initially has a rectangular form with a shadow around it. The rectangular form and the shadow disappear once the transition has completed, but the shape can be seen momentarily.</p>
<p>Unfortunately, I wasn't able to find a way to set a capsule shape, to prevent the rectangular shape from happening. In particular, applying <code>.buttonBorderShape(.capsule)</code> doesn't help. So a possible workaround here is to apply a clip shape. This is not ideal, especially since it also clips the shadow, but it may be preferable to seeing the rectangular artefact.</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>NavigationStack {
    VStack {
        Menu(&quot;Menu&quot;) {
            ForEach(itemsToList, id: \.self) { item in
                Button(item) {}
            }
        }
        .glassEffect()
        .buttonStyle(.glass)
        .clipShape(.capsule)
    }
}
</code></pre>
